# SPRINT 7: Paribhasha -- Three Intentional Violations
#
# Each block below is a SEPARATE program. The compiler must reject
# each one independently, emitting zero bytes. The pipeline tests
# each violation in isolation. The rejection is the proof.
#
# P1: Anuvrtti-at-start
#     First instruction has no Karaka (no prior context to inherit).
#
# P2: Lopa-on-unwritten
#     Lopa targets Vak (0x30) which was never written in this stream.
#
# P3: Vrddhi-on-Siddha
#     Ring 0 privilege applied to Vak bus (0x30, Siddha < 0x50).
#     Vrddhi is only valid for Asiddha targets (>= 0x50).
#     The Karaka "vaagyanthraiah" (vaagg + yantra + Ring-0 instrumental)
#     encodes this structurally invalid combination.

# --- VIOLATION P1: Anuvrtti-at-start ---
# Compile: python -B -c "
#   import importlib.util; spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
#     'sc', 'src/utils/paninian_compiler.py')
#   mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
#   mod.PaninianFormalCompiler().compile_source(open('paribhasha_core.pvm').read())"
# Expected: ParibhashaError P1

# P1 violation program:
# tantrashastram {
#     likhati .            <-- no Karaka: Anuvrtti as first statement
# }

# --- VIOLATION P2: Lopa-on-unwritten ---
# P2 violation program:
# tantrashastram {
#     vacham lopah .       <-- Vak never written; Lopa is incoherent
# }

# --- VIOLATION P3: Vrddhi-on-Siddha ---
# P3 violation program:
# tantrashastram {
#     vaagyanthraiah likhati .   <-- Ring 0 on Siddha target (0x30 < 0x50)
# }
#
# NOTE: The pipeline embeds the three violation programs as UTF-8 inline
# strings in run_rv32_paribhasha_pipeline.py (Part A). This file is the
# human-readable documentation of what those programs contain.
